- How would you adapt for at-most-k windows instead of exactly k?
- How would you reconstruct the actual windows efficiently and handle negative values?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests DP with a non-adjacent selection constraint and exact count, forcing clarity on state definition and reconstruction.

## Key Concepts
- DP on two dimensions (i, t) with take/skip choices. 
- Non-adjacency enforced by using i-2 when taking i. 
- Backtracking recovers chosen indices; space optimization possible.

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxKNonAdjacent(A, k) {
  const n = A.length;
  const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
  const take = Array.from({length: n+1}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(false));
  for(let i=0;i<=n;i++) dp[i][0]=0;
  for(let i=1;i<=n;i++){
    for(let t=1;t<=k;t++){
      dp[i][t] = dp[i-1][t];
      const val = (i>=2 ? dp[i-2][t-1] : (t===1 ? 0 : -Infinity)) + A[i-1];
      if(val > dp[i][t]){ dp[i][t] = val; take[i][t] = true; }
    }
  }
  let res = [], i=n, t=k;
  while(i>0 && t>0){
    if(take[i][t]){ res.push(i); i-=2; t--; } else { i--; }
  }
  res.reverse();
  return {sum: dp[n][k], indices: res};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt for at-most k selections? 
- How would you optimize space to O(k)?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests nuanced DP on partitioning with range-dependent block costs, requiring problem decomposition, state design, and path reconstruction.

## Key Concepts
- DP over prefixes and block counts
- Efficient range max computation in transitions
- Path reconstruction via parent pointers

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxScore(nums, m) {
  const n = nums.length;
  const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, ()=> Array(m+1).fill(-Infinity));
  const par = Array.from({length: n+1}, ()=> Array(m+1).fill(-1));
  dp[0][0] = 0;
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let t = 1; t <= Math.min(i, m); t++) {
      let curMax = -Infinity;
      for (let j = i-1; j >= t-1; j--) {
        curMax = Math.max(curMax, nums[j]);
        const val = dp[j][t-1] + curMax * (i - j);
        if (val > dp[i][t]) {
          dp[i][t] = val;
          par[i][t] = j;
        }
      }
    }
  }
  // Reconstruct endpoints
  const ends = [];
  let i = n, t = m;
  while (t > 0) {
    ends.push(i);
    i = par[i][t];
    t--;
  }
  return {score: dp[n][m], ends: ends.reverse()};
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Realistic sprint planning with bounded weekly windows; tests ability to form and optimize DP with small constant branching and reconstruction.

## Key Concepts
- Prefix sums for fast range totals
- DP with fixed window length (2..7)
- Path reconstruction via parent pointers
- Time O(n) with a small constant, Space O(n)

## Code Example
```javascript
function minWeeklyCost(A, W) {
  const n = A.length;
  const pref = new Array(n+1).fill(0);
  for (let i=1;i<=n;i++) pref[i] = pref[i-1] + A[i-1];
  const INF = 1e18;
  const dp = new Array(n+1).fill(INF);
  const par = new Array(n+1).fill(-1);
  dp[0] = 0;
  for (let i=2; i<=n; i++) {
    for (let k=2; k<=7; k++) {
      if (i-k < 0) break;
      const sum = pref[i] - pref[i-k];
      const cost = dp[i-k] + (sum - W)*(sum - W);
      if (cost < dp[i]) { dp[i] = cost; par[i] = i-k; }
    }
  }
  // reconstruction would follow using par[]
  return {cost: dp[n], prev: par[n]};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle negative A values if allowed? 
- What changes if weeks must start on a specific weekday?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design DP over partitions with a non-linear segment value, including reconstruction of segments. Encourages using prefix sums for fast segment sums and careful indexing to enforce exactly k non-empty contiguous blocks. Also invites discussion of time/space trade-offs and potential optimizations.

## Key Concepts

- Prefix sums for quick segment sums
- DP over partitions with exact k blocks
- Reconstructing the chosen boundaries via a prev table

## Code Example

```javascript
function maxPartition(A, k, c){
  const n = A.length;
  const P = new Array(n+1).fill(0);
  for(let i=1;i<=n;i++) P[i] = P[i-1] + A[i-1];
  const dp = Array.from({length:n+1},()=>new Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
  const prev = Array.from({length:n+1},()=>new Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  dp[0][0] = 0;
  for(let i=1;i<=n;i++) dp[i][1] = (P[i]-P[0])**2 - c, prev[i][1] = 0;
  for(let t=2;t<=k;t++){
    for(let i=t;i<=n;i++){
      for(let j=t-1;j<=i-1;j++){
        const val = dp[j][t-1] + (P[i]-P[j])**2 - c;
        if(val > dp[i][t]){ dp[i][t] = val; prev[i][t] = j; }
      }
    }
  }
  // recover endpoints via backtracking
  return {best: dp[n][k], prev};
}
``` 

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you reconstruct the actual segment boundaries from prev?
- Could you optimize to O(nk) with advanced DP techniques or convex hull tricks?
- How would changes to c (per-segment cost) affect the optimal k if it’s not fixed?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests a three-dimensional DP that models constrained path planning with a binary obstacle grid and a budget of unblocks. Requires careful state definition, transitions, and path reconstruction. 

## Key Concepts
- DP with an extra dimension for unblocks (t)
- Handling blocked vs unblocked cells in transitions
- Path reconstruction from 3D DP table
- Time/space trade-offs and rolling optimization

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxPath(grid, blocked, k) {
  const n = grid.length, m = grid[0].length;
  const NEG = -1e15;
  const dp = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array.from({length: m}, () => Array(k+1).fill(NEG)));
  if (!blocked[0][0]) dp[0][0][0] = grid[0][0];
  else if (k > 0) dp[0][0][1] = grid[0][0];
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    for (let j = 0; j < m; j++) {
      for (let t = 0; t <= k; t++) {
        const val = grid[i][j];
        if (i > 0) {
          dp[i][j][t] = Math.max(dp[i][j][t], dp[i-1][j][t] + val * (blocked[i][j] ? 0 : 1));
          if (blocked[i][j] && t > 0) dp[i][j][t] = Math.max(dp[i][j][t], dp[i-1][j][t-1] + val);
        }
        if (j > 0) {
          dp[i][j][t] = Math.max(dp[i][j][t], dp[i][j-1][t] + val * (blocked[i][j] ? 0 : 1));
          if (blocked[i][j] && t > 0) dp[i][j][t] = Math.max(dp[i][j][t], dp[i][j-1][t-1] + val);
        }
      }
    }
  }
  return dp[n-1][m-1][0];
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you recover the actual path from the 3D DP and handle ties?
- What optimizations would you apply for large grids or memory constraints?

