## How It Works

Concise technical explanation of the mechanism.

## Key Points

- **DP State**: local[k], global[k] track ending vs non-ending subarrays
- **Transition**: local[k] = max(local[k]+a[i], global[k-1]+a[i]); global[k] = max(global[k], local[k])
- **Edge Handling**: cap K to n; initialize -inf; handles negative values

## Example

```javascript
function maxRevenue(A, K) {
  const n = A.length;
  if (n === 0 || K <= 0) return 0;
  K = Math.min(K, n);
  const global = new Array(K+1).fill(-Infinity);
  const local = new Array(K+1).fill(-Infinity);
  global[0] = 0;
  for (let x of A) {
    for (let k = K; k >= 1; k--) {
      local[k] = Math.max((local[k] !== -Infinity ? local[k] + x : -Infinity), global[k-1] + x);
      global[k] = Math.max(global[k], local[k]);
    }
  }
  return global[K];
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests a DP over partitioned subarrays with a non-standard per-subarray score and reconstruction.

## Key Concepts
- DP on non-overlapping subarrays
- Prefix sums and range minimums
- Path reconstruction and base cases
- Complexity: O(n^2 k) time, O(nk) space

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxScore(A, k){
  const n = A.length; const P=[0]; for(let i=0;i<n;i++) P[i+1]=P[i]+A[i];
  const dp=Array.from({length:n+1},()=>Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
  const prev=Array.from({length:n+1},()=>Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  for(let i=1;i<=n;i++) dp[i][1]=P[i]-Math.min(...A.slice(0,i));
  for(let t=2;t<=k;t++){
    for(let i=t;i<=n;i++){
      for(let p=t-1;p<=i-1;p++){
        const minVal=Math.min(...A.slice(p,i));
        const val=dp[p][t-1]+(P[i]-P[p])-minVal;
        if(val>dp[i][t]){ dp[i][t]=val; prev[i][t]=p; }
      }
    }
  }
  // reconstruction would trace back using prev
  return dp[n][k];
}
```

